      "problem": "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}{e}^{x/y},$ find $∇f(x,y).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x}{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}){e}^{x/y} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{x/y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x}{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}){e}^{x/y} \\\\  & =\\frac{y-x}{xy}{e}^{x/y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{x/y}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y-x}{xy}{e}^{x/y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
